We need to assert that if the input array a is not null, then the returned array (which is a) is also not null.

But note that the method may reassign a to a new array if a.length < size. In that case, a is assigned a new array, which will also be non-null (new instance). If a is not null initially, after possible reassignment it remains non-null (since new array is non-null). So the spec: (\old(a != null) => \result != null). However we need to refer to input array before method: a is a parameter; but after possibly reassigning, a variable may change, but we want to assert that the resulting array (the return value) is also not null, given that the input array (original) is not null. Use \old(a) to refer to original argument. So assert: (\old(a) != null) => \result != null.

